      "chapterNumber": 1,
      "headline": "Prologue: Overcoming System Blindness",
      "keyInsight": "We love blaming personalities when things go sideways at work. If a project derails, we assume the manager is incompetent or the teammate is lazy.\n\nBarry Oshry calls this system blindness. When you put ordinary, kind humans into specific structural positions, predictable friction erupts on autopilot.\n\nImagine two drivers honking furiously at a chaotic, blind four-way intersection with zero traffic signals. Are both drivers naturally toxic, rude people?\n\nAbsolutely not. The invisible road design forces them into immediate conflict.\n\nThe paradox of work life is that our biggest emotional clashes are rarely personal. We believe we are fighting difficult individuals, but we are actually bumping into invisible walls built into the organizational architecture.\n\nWhen you cure system blindness, finger-pointing dissolves. You stop asking who broke the machine and start looking at how the machine shapes human behavior.",
      "content": "Step into any bustling office kitchen, and you will hear familiar complaints. People swear their executives are out of touch, while executives grumble that staff lack initiative. It feels intensely personal, bitter, and exhausting.\n\nYet if you swapped everyone into each other's jobs tomorrow, the exact same complaints would reappear within weeks. We are blind to the invisible currents shaping how we react to one another.\n\nThink of a busy harbor during an incoming ocean tide. Small dinghies drift, heavy cargo ships pull against their anchors, and docks creak under pressure. If you only look at individual boats, you might think each captain is steering erratically.\n\nIn reality, a powerful subterranean swell pushes every vessel at once. Organizations operate under identical hidden currents. When we cannot see the system, we turn structural friction into personal feuds.\n\nOvercoming system blindness means stepping back from personal drama to spot the wider field of motion. You stop asking what is wrong with your stubborn coworker. Instead, you begin mapping the hidden pressures that make their behavior feel totally rational to them.\n\nOnce you see the room as an interconnected ecosystem, blame turns into genuine curiosity.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Before you blame someone's personality, examine the invisible structure steering their daily choices.",
